 "note": "Changed rows only. Held ids are sorted under held_by_reason; reasons may overlap. Round 3 scope is the union of prior state_zone_conflict_review and zone_outside_country.",
 "methods": [
  "Strict containment in exactly one TBB feature at stored WGS84 point. Test ALL boundary chunks against a conservative 2010m enclosing lon/lat box (110000m/degree, cosine at poleward box edge). If any intersect, clip to box, densify to <=0.0001 degree and project to point-centred WGS84 azimuthal equidistant; hold clearance <=2010m (10m safety margin). Coastline and holes count as borders. No candidate escapes a 2km zone-border disk. Compare rules by IANA Link targets, but write only literal zone.tab entries for the row country. Preserve a stored country-listed name when its rules match the polygon. Otherwise prefer an exact country-listed polygon name, then a unique country-listed zone sharing its Link target; hold absent or ambiguous mappings. Conservatively hold any mismatch with stored state timezone, unresolved original identities and special-territory screens. State scalar mismatch is a review hold, not proof the scalar is right. Round 3: scope is the union of the two prior conflict groups only. Scalar state timezone mismatch does not veto a country-compatible point proof. Use the own ISO 3166-1 territory code for policy-listed multi-level states; keep all other original hold reasons, all unresolved identities and Manitoba. No alias-only changes. Reuse hashed Round 2 point/border proofs only after checking the stored inputs; independently verify geodesic disks for every change."
 ],
